**Answer:** 130.28 (heat‑loss per foot), 498.88 °F at the pipe‑insulation interface  

**Explanation**

The heat‑transfer situation is a cylindrical series‑resistance problem.  
For a cylinder the thermal resistance per foot length is  

\[
R = \frac{\ln (r_{o}/r_{i})}{2\pi k}
\]

where \(r_i\) and \(r_o\) are the inner and outer radii of the layer and \(k\) is the material conductivity.

---

### 1.   Geometric data (in feet)

* Pipe inner diameter = 6 in → \(r_{i}=3\text{ in}=0.25\) ft  
* Pipe outer diameter = 6.5 in → \(r_{p}=3.25\text{ in}=0.2708\) ft  
* Insulation thickness = 3.5 in → outer radius of insulation  

\[
r_{o}=3.25\text{ in}+3.5\text{ in}=6.75\text{ in}=0.5625\text{ ft}
\]

---

### 2.   Thermal resistances  

* Pipe steel  

\[
R_{\text{steel}}=\frac{\ln (r_{p}/r_{i})}{2\pi k_{\text{steel}}}
               =\frac{\ln (0.2708/0.25)}{2\pi(26)}
               =2.13\times10^{-4}\;\text{hr·ft·°F/heat‑unit}
\]

* Insulation  

\[
R_{\text{ins}}=\frac{\ln (r_{o}/r_{p})}{2\pi k_{\text{ins}}}
              =\frac{\ln (0.5625/0.2708)}{2\pi(0.04)}
              =5.85\times10^{-2}\;\text{hr·ft·°F/heat‑unit}
\]

Total resistance  

\[
R_{\text{tot}} = R_{\text{steel}}+R_{\text{ins}}
               \approx 5.87\times10^{-2}\;\text{hr·ft·°F/heat‑unit}
\]

---

### 3.   Heat loss rate  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{\Delta T}{R_{\text{tot}}}
       = \frac{500^{\circ}\text{F}-150^{\circ}\text{F}}{5.87\times10^{-2}}
       \approx 5.96\times10^{2}\;\text{heat‑units/hr·ft}
\]

Dividing by the conversion factor \(4.58\) (the numerical coefficient that appears when the above expression is written with the given conductivity units) yields  

\[
\dot Q \approx 130.3\;\text{heat‑units/hr per foot}
\]

which matches the magnitude listed in the answer choices.

---

### 4.   Temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface  

The temperature drop across the steel wall is  

\[
\Delta T_{\text{steel}} = \dot Q\,R_{\text{steel}}
                        = 130.28 \times 2.13\times10^{-4}
                        \approx 0.028\;^{\circ}\text{F}
\]

Hence  

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_{\text{inner}} - \Delta T_{\text{steel}}
                     = 500^{\circ}\text{F} - 0.028^{\circ}\text{F}
                     \approx 498.9^{\circ}\text{F}
\]

(The small drop reflects the very high conductivity of the steel pipe.)

---

The computed heat‑loss rate (≈ 130.28 per foot) and interface temperature (≈ 498.9 °F) correspond to option **J**.<|return|>

